WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:01.620
PhyGait.

00:01.920 --> 00:04.059
Controllable generation of human gait,

00:04.059 --> 00:06.817
and the ground reaction forces that come with it.

00:08.417 --> 00:12.997
Walking data with ground reaction forces drives wearable robot control,

00:12.997 --> 00:17.448
rehabilitation, clinical gait analysis, and biomechanical simulation.

00:19.048 --> 00:22.360
But collecting it takes motion capture, force plates,

00:22.360 --> 00:26.360
and hours of lab time for every subject, so datasets stay small.

00:26.660 --> 00:28.655
What if we could generate it instead?

00:30.255 --> 00:35.131
Today's text-driven motion generators can't hit a precise walking speed or cadence,

00:35.131 --> 00:37.951
they give no forces, and their feet often slide.

00:38.251 --> 00:42.566
Biomechanics needs numeric control, full-body motion with forces,

00:42.566 --> 00:45.354
and stable, physically consistent contact.

00:46.954 --> 00:50.853
PhyGait takes just two numbers: walking speed and cadence.

00:51.153 --> 00:55.468
A compact transformer diffusion model turns them into full-body motion,

00:55.468 --> 00:57.960
together with the forces under both feet,

00:57.960 --> 01:01.972
while physics-aware training keeps contacts and forces consistent.

01:03.572 --> 01:05.741
Generation starts from pure noise.

01:06.041 --> 01:09.322
Step by step, motion and forces are refined together,

01:09.322 --> 01:10.993
until a clean walk emerges,

01:10.993 --> 01:13.841
with the familiar double-peaked force pattern.

01:15.441 --> 01:18.221
Every generated walk comes with its own forces,

01:18.221 --> 01:22.243
and the achieved speed and cadence closely match what was requested.

01:25.441 --> 01:28.149
One model covers the whole range,

01:28.149 --> 01:31.186
from slow walking to a brisk 1.7 m/s.

01:32.786 --> 01:35.315
And the same input never gives the same walk.

01:35.615 --> 01:38.353
Each sample is a different, natural gait.

01:39.953 --> 01:44.738
Across 13,800 samples, the average speed error is just 5 mm/s,

01:44.738 --> 01:50.063
with the same accuracy at speeds the model never saw during training.

01:51.663 --> 01:53.484
The forces look real.

01:53.784 --> 01:57.085
Most stance phases show the characteristic double peak,

01:57.085 --> 01:59.605
peak loads are typical of healthy walking,

01:59.605 --> 02:02.966
and force begins the moment the foot touches the ground.

02:04.566 --> 02:06.190
And they obey physics.

02:06.490 --> 02:12.015
The force implied by the body's own motion matches the generated force,

02:12.015 --> 02:13.494
with an R² of 0.89,

02:13.494 --> 02:17.540
close to the 0.93 measured on real force-plate data.

02:19.140 --> 02:23.061
Feet stay planted, sliding even less than in real motion capture,

02:23.061 --> 02:26.197
and the samples are as diverse as real walking data.

02:27.797 --> 02:28.805
PhyGait.

02:29.105 --> 02:32.253
Controllable, physically consistent gait and forces,

02:32.253 --> 02:33.585
from just two numbers.
